==French==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|fr}} {{term|bidon||small portable container, sealed and made of wood or metal|lang=frm}}, of {{etyl|gem|fr}} origin, from {{etyl|non|fr}} *{{term||bida, *biða|container, vessel|lang=non}}, from {{proto|Germanic|bidōn||bidjan|tub, vessel, vat|lang=fr}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|bʰidʰ-|pot, bucket, barrel|lang=fr}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|title=|bʰeidʰ-|to weave, bind|lang=fr}}. Cognate with {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|biða||milkbucket|lang=is}}, {{etyl|no|-}} {{term|bide||churn|lang=no}}, {{etyl|no|-}} {{term|bidne||vessel|lang=no}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{term|fidelia|fidēlia|clay or glass vessel|lang=la}}, {{etyl|grc|-}} {{term|πίθος||tr=píthos|wine barrel|lang=grc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|Fr-bidon.ogg|audio}}
* {{IPA|/bi.dɔ̃/|lang=fr}}

===Noun===
{{fr-noun|m}}

# [[can]], [[tin]], [[canister]]
# (''childish language'') [[tummy]]

===Adjective===
{{fr-adj|inv=yes|type=inv}}

# {{slang|lang=fr}} [[rigged]]
#: La course de chevaux est '''bidon'''.
# {{slang|lang=fr}} [[phoney]], [[sham]]
#: Ces cris et ces pleurs sont '''bidon''' puisqu'elle ne l'aimait pas.
# {{slang|lang=fr}} [[crap]]
#: Ce film est complètement '''bidon'''.

===Derived terms===
* {{l|fr|bidonville}}

===Anagrams===
* [[bondi#French|bondi]]

